/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_Overview
 *
 * Foundation supports three main usage modes:
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildSourceMode Source Mode
 *
 * Source mode uses the repository directly.
 *
 * This is the default mode for:
 *
 * - Arduino,
 * - development,
 * - debugging,
 * - changing feature macros,
 * - modifying Foundation internals.
 *
 * In source mode, Foundation remains fully configurable through compile-time macros.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildDesktopDevelopment Desktop Development
 *
 * For a Windows, Linux, or macOS application, use CMake `FetchContent` to make
 * Foundation part of the application's build. This is the supported day-to-day
 * development path: Foundation and CPSTL compile with the application's selected
 * compiler, architecture, configuration, and C++ runtime. There is no separate
 * Foundation export, installation, or `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` to configure.
 *
 * Add this to the application's top-level `CMakeLists.txt` before linking the
 * application target:
 *
 * @code{.cmake}
 * include(FetchContent)
 *
 * FetchContent_Declare(Foundation
 *   GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation.git
 *   GIT_TAG v0.0.1
 * )
 * FetchContent_MakeAvailable(Foundation)
 *
 * target_link_libraries(MyApplication PRIVATE Foundation::Foundation)
 * @endcode
 *
 * The first configure downloads the pinned Foundation revision and its CPSTL
 * dependency into the application's build tree; later builds reuse CMake's
 * download cache. Configure the application normally for the platform you are
 * developing on. The platform pages document the corresponding compiler and
 * architecture choices.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildFullPrecompiledMode Full Precompiled Mode
 *
 * Full precompiled mode builds Foundation with all supported modules enabled.
 *
 * This mode is intended for:
 *
 * - controlled Release distribution,
 * - users that need a ready-to-link binary for one exact target,
 * - generic full builds for known toolchains.
 *
 * A full build is larger, but easier to consume.
 * The official export contains the Release library only and remains specific to
 * its preset's compiler and architecture. Debug is built from source when needed.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildCustomPrecompiledMode Custom Precompiled Mode
 *
 * Custom precompiled mode builds Foundation for a specific target, toolchain,
 * architecture, ABI, and feature set.
 *
 * This mode is intended for:
 *
 * - embedded targets,
 * - memory-constrained systems,
 * - production builds,
 * - projects that only need a subset of Foundation.
 *
 * The selected modules are compiled into the package and recorded in
 * `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake`. A different feature selection is a different
 * compatibility product even when the target preset itself is unchanged.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildRule General Rule
 *
 * If macros change the compiled implementation, build a custom library.
 * For normal desktop development, use source mode through `FetchContent`.
 * If the project needs maximum flexibility, use source mode.
 * Use a precompiled full build only when distributing a controlled Release
 * binary for its exact target identity.
 * Always validate a precompiled package with a separate consumer using the same
 * target toolchain and ABI.
 */
